我听到你所说的话了。

00:26:19.849 --> 00:26:24.369
Well, okay, I'll share why those exchanges worked, I think.
好吧，那我就分享一下，为什么这些交流会起作用，我想。

00:26:24.369 --> 00:26:31.769
Because I said, this is very hurtful to me, because sometimes it would be with like fans of how I met your mother even, or people that were engaging with me on some other issue.
因为我说：“这对我很伤人，因为有时候对方甚至会是《老爸老妈浪漫史》的粉丝，或者是在其他问题上和我交流的人。”

00:26:31.769 --> 00:26:38.150
And I'd say, look, if you like any of the work I've done, I just want you to know, I am a human being.
我会说：“听着，如果你喜欢我做过的任何作品，我只想让你知道，我是一个人。”

00:26:38.150 --> 00:26:40.829
I love my son more than anything in the world.
我爱我的儿子胜过世上的一切。

00:26:40.829 --> 00:26:47.549
And this word is a slur against an aspect of his humanity and his identity and a journey he's on.
而这个词是对他人性中某个方面、他的身份认同以及他正在经历的人生旅程的一种污蔑。

00:26:47.549 --> 00:26:58.509
think about it, he didn't ask for extra learning challenges in life, he didn't want that, but he has that and he has to overcome that and he deserves respect for that.
你想想看，他并不想要生活中额外的学习挑战，他并不想要这些，但他确实遇到了，他必须克服这些，他理应为此受到尊重。

00:26:58.509 --> 00:27:13.312
And to use words, these lazy prepackaged words that equate having a disability with the abstract concept of just stupidity, worthlessness, notlessness, if you're saying those two things are the same, they're not.
而使用这些词，这些偷懒的、现成的、把残疾等同于愚蠢、毫无价值、一无是处等抽象概念的词汇——如果你认为这两者是一回事，那它们根本不是一回事。

00:27:13.312 --> 00:27:18.269
You're really hurting my feelings because my son is so important to me.
你真的深深伤害了我的感情，因为我的儿子对我来说太重要了。

00:27:18.269 --> 00:27:22.622
So the times I've made it personal are the times when I've had to work.
所以，只有当我把事情上升到个人层面时，我才取得过成效。

00:27:22.622 --> 00:27:38.561
But then if you make it personal and the person still says, F you, you're the R word, it hurts even more because now you feel like you've opened your heart to that person and like it didn't work and they just sort of like stabbed the dagger in a little deeper.
但后来，如果你把它变成个人问题，而对方仍然说：“去你的，你就是那个R开头的词”，这会让人觉得更痛，因为现在你会觉得你向那个人敞开了心扉，结果却不管用，他们只是像往你胸口更深地捅了一刀。

00:27:38.561 --> 00:27:42.361
But the times it's worked are the times I've made it personal.
但起作用的时候，都是因为我把它变成了个人层面的交流。

00:27:42.361 --> 00:27:49.166
I see this word coming up a lot online, on social media, and it's thrown around in political discussions a lot.
我看到这个词在网上、在社交媒体上经常出现，而且在政治讨论中也被大量抛出来。

00:27:49.166 --> 00:27:54.315
I see it a lot in that sort of way where people say, libitard, you know what I mean?
我经常看到这种用法，比如有人说“白左白痴”（libtard），你知道我的意思吗？

00:27:54.315 --> 00:27:54.867
Equating.
把它们划等号。

00:27:54.867 --> 00:28:01.488
They're sort of just like, let me take what I perceive to be your political beliefs and equate it with that.
他们差不多就是：“让我把你所持的政治信仰拿来，然后跟那个词画上等号。”

00:28:01.488 --> 00:28:05.533
I see all these different ways people are trying to bring it back.
我看到人们正试图用各种不同的方式把这个词重新翻出来用。

00:28:05.533 --> 00:28:14.238
And I think to myself, if they had somebody in their life that they loved, who had a disability and said to them, it really hurts my feelings.
我心里就在想，如果他们生命中也有一个他们所爱的人，身有残疾，并且对他们说：“这真的很伤我的感情。”

00:28:14.238 --> 00:28:17.978
When you use this, it really diminishes me and disparages me.
“当你使用这个词时，这真的让我感到被贬低和轻蔑。”

00:28:17.978 --> 00:28:21.901
That might be the only way to get through to somebody like that.
这可能是唯一能真正触及那种人的方法了。

00:28:21.901 --> 00:28:31.710
I think there's a lot, I notice this phenomenon a lot where people only understand something abstract when it comes into their life in reality and specifically.
我觉得有很多——我注意到这样一个现象，很多人只有在现实生活中具体地接触到某种事物时，才能理解原本抽象的东西。

00:28:31.710 --> 00:28:43.750
And so the challenge seems to be if somebody doesn't have that in their life, you have to talk about it in a way that makes it visceral, emotional, and real to them.
所以，面临的挑战似乎在于，如果某个人的生活中没有这种经历，你就必须以一种让他们感同身受、充满情绪且真实可感的方式来谈论它。

00:28:43.750 --> 00:28:47.309
And that takes a lot of trust because you're opening something up in your heart.
这需要极大的信任，因为你在向别人敞开你内心深处最柔软的部分。

00:28:47.309 --> 00:28:59.950
If I talk about my son to somebody and they still insult me with the R word and say, you're just woke and screw off, that hurts more because I shared something of myself with that person.
如果我跟某个人谈起我的儿子，而他们仍然用那个R开头的词来侮辱我，说“你就是太觉醒了，滚一边去”，这会让我更加痛心，因为我曾把真实的自己分享给那个人。

00:28:59.950 --> 00:29:07.522
you know, I think you have to have an open heart and you have to try to talk to people about how it feels and that we are all human beings.
你知道，我认为你必须敞开心扉，你必须试着跟别人谈谈这种感受，谈谈我们所有人都是人。

00:29:07.522 --> 00:29:08.503
We all have value.
我们都有价值。

00:29:08.503 --> 00:29:10.083
This is a word that devalues.
而这是一个在贬低价值的词。

00:29:10.083 --> 00:29:12.371
This is a way of talking about disability.
这是一种谈论残疾的方式。

00:29:12.371 --> 00:29:13.461
The devalues people.
它在贬低残疾人。

00:29:13.461 --> 00:29:15.095
People disabilities are human.
残疾人也是人。

00:29:15.095 --> 00:29:21.470
They have parents and families who are humans and love them and deserve respect and decency and humanity and dignity.
他们有父母和家人，这些父母和家人都是活生生的人，深爱着他们，理应得到尊重、体面、仁慈和尊严。

00:29:23.069 --> 00:29:31.090
And it's I just think my the only times that have felt a level of success and engaging with people is when I make it personal to them in some way.
而且我觉得，我唯一感到在与人交流中取得了一定成效的时刻，都是当我以某种方式将事情与他们个人产生关联的时候。

00:29:31.090 --> 00:29:36.913
And of course, I've now written a whole novel that I hope does that, and I hope it doesn't in a funny way.
当然，我现在写了一整部小说，我希望它能达到这个效果，而且我希望它是以一种有趣的方式做到的。

00:29:36.913 --> 00:29:51.306
It's mostly a comedic novel, and I find that comedy, the right kind of comedy, can disarm people and open them up, and I hope that I found a way of writing about it in my book that might open a few minds and might make people realize, oh, I see why this matters.
它主要是一部喜剧小说，我发现喜剧，那种正确的喜剧，可以卸下人们的防备并让他们敞开心扉，我希望我在书中找到了一种写作方式，能够打开一些人的心智，让他们意识到：“噢，我明白了为什么这很重要。”

00:29:51.306 --> 00:29:52.349
I see what this is.
我明白了这究竟是怎么一回事。

00:29:52.349 --> 00:30:14.890
This makes so much sort of sense to me as a constellation of things where it's like, if the comedy that punches down and is shock value and that's isolating, right, you're pushing people out, the kind of things that you create pull people in and it also like when you say, because I can really very much the feeling of having to make the thing that hurts personal in order to have other people care.
对我来说，这就像是一系列相互关联的事物，一下子变得非常合情合理：如果那种向下开玩笑、靠制造震惊效果、把人边缘化的喜剧——对吧，你在把人往外推；而你所创作的那类作品则能把人拉近。这也让我想起，因为我太能体会那种必须把痛苦的事情个人化，才能让别人去在乎的心情了。

00:30:14.890 --> 00:30:23.589
Like one of the questions that comes to me over and over and over as I do this work is, how do you get people to care about things that don't affect them?
就像我在做这方面工作时，一遍又一遍、反反复复盘旋在我脑海中的一个问题是：你如何让人们去在乎那些与他们无关的事情？

00:30:23.589 --> 00:30:31.589
And one of the only ways that I've seen that happen is through media, is through art, TV, books.
而我所见过的唯一途径之一就是通过媒体，通过艺术、电视和书籍。

00:30:31.589 --> 00:30:37.589
You become intimately acquainted with characters that are not, you know, real people, but they feel very real to you.
你会与那些角色建立起亲密的熟悉感，他们虽然不是真实的人，但对你来说却感觉非常真实。

00:30:37.589 --> 00:30:48.670
And when you talk about writing the book, it's almost like, because I do feel like as people in the disability community, We do what you're saying.
当谈到写这本书时，几乎就像是——因为我觉得作为残疾人社群的一员，我们正在做着你所说的事情。

00:30:48.670 --> 00:30:52.589
We have to kind of lay ourselves bare over and over and over and say, like, please care.
我们不得不一次又一次、一遍又一遍地将自己赤裸裸地展现出来，说：“请在乎一下吧。”

00:30:52.589 --> 00:30:54.089
Please care about this.
“请在乎这件事吧。”

00:30:54.089 --> 00:30:54.930
Please, please.
“求求你们了。”

00:30:54.930 --> 00:30:55.869
And it's exhausting.
这太让人精疲力竭了。

00:30:55.869 --> 00:30:56.710
Right?
对吧？

00:30:56.710 --> 00:30:57.549
It's emotionally amazing.
这在情感上太消耗了。

00:30:57.549 --> 00:31:00.470
To plead it, you can do it sometimes, but there's almost no other way.
去乞求别人的关注，你偶尔能做到，但几乎别无他法。

00:31:00.470 --> 00:31:01.309
Right.
没错。

00:31:01.309 --> 00:31:05.390
So it's like you have to like kind of like throw up in your chest and be like, look, I'm a person.
所以这就像是你必须强忍着恶心，然后说：“听着，我也是个人。”

00:31:05.390 --> 00:31:11.309
Just give some amount of a, give a care, as the seveners say, give a care.
哪怕给一点儿，正如俚语所说，在乎一下吧。

00:31:11.309 --> 00:31:13.829
And you know, but like that wears you out, it's tiring.
你知道的，但这种事会把人掏空，太累人了。

00:31:13.829 --> 00:31:16.955
And you have to do it on a lot of fronts in a lot of ways.
而且你不得不在很多方面、以许多不同的方式去这样做。

00:31:16.955 --> 00:31:31.990
And for me, when I see something like your book, or like that piece, which was so beautiful in the globe, you know, there's this beautiful level of removal, you know, you're making people care, you're making it personal personal, excuse me, you're making it personal through art.
对我而言，当我看到诸如你的书，或者像那篇发表在《环球报》上、如此美丽的文章时，你知道，这里有一种美妙的间离感，你知道的，你正在让人们去在乎，你正在通过艺术将它个人化——不好意思，通过艺术让它与个人产生联系。

00:31:31.990 --> 00:31:37.019
So it's almost like you have a screen in front of you, you're still, but you're still bringing people in.
所以这几乎就像你的面前有一块屏幕，你依然在这么做，但你依然把人们吸引了过来。

00:31:37.019 --> 00:31:37.519
Yeah.
是的。

00:31:37.519 --> 00:31:39.914
Which, I don't know, what do you think about that?
对此，我不知道，你怎么看？

00:31:39.914 --> 00:31:46.716
Like what responsibility to media, to art have in this world because it feels to me like that's the biggest stage you can do this possibility.
比如，媒体和艺术在这个世界上承担着怎样的责任？因为在我看来，这是你推动这种可能性所能登上的最大舞台。

00:31:46.716 --> 00:31:48.968
That's why I feel like we have to be out there.
这就是为什么我觉得我们必须发声。

00:31:48.968 --> 00:31:51.650
That's why I'm so glad you're you're doing this podcast.
这也是为什么我如此高兴你正在做这个播客。

00:31:51.650 --> 00:31:53.853
That's why I'm trying to write stuff about it.
这也是为什么我一直在试着写一些关于这方面的内容。

00:31:53.853 --> 00:31:55.530
I think it's a huge responsibility.
我认为这是一项巨大的责任。

00:31:55.269 --> 00:32:09.863
I think when people can be engaged on a human level, the way that an honest conversation in this podcast space could do, the way that I hope my book can do, the way I hope that piece out for the Boston Globe did, it has to be on a human level.
我认为，当人们能够在人类层面上进行互动时，就像这个播客空间里的一场真诚对话所能做到的那样，就像我希望我的书所能做到的那样，就像我希望《波士顿环球报》的那篇文章所能做到的那样，它必须在人类的层面上展开。

00:32:09.863 --> 00:32:14.907
I think I think when it's abstract, people can swat it away and it remains abstract.
我觉得，当它只是抽象概念时，人们就会把它挥开，而它也依然只是个抽象概念。

00:32:14.907 --> 00:32:17.669
So much of human life now in 2025 is abstract.
在如今的2025年，人类生活中的许多事情都是抽象的。

00:32:18.549 --> 00:32:19.371
It's digital.
它是数字化的。

00:32:19.371 --> 00:32:21.142
It feels at a remove, right?
它让人感觉隔着一段距离，对吧？

00:32:21.142 --> 00:32:22.469
It doesn't feel real.
它感觉很不真实。

00:32:22.469 --> 00:32:24.809
And so it's how do you make it human?
所以问题是，你如何让它变得有人情味？

00:32:24.809 --> 00:32:26.579
How do you make it visceral?
你如何让它感同身受？

00:32:26.579 --> 00:32:28.792
How do you give it a beating heart?
你如何赋予它一颗跳动的心？

00:32:28.792 --> 00:32:31.194
And I think that's the huge challenge.
我认为这才是巨大的挑战。

00:32:31.194 --> 00:32:45.609
That Boston Globe piece I wrote was kind of admitted to my own ignorance a bit in ways that I've done in this interview too, where it sort of takes you through this journey of like I became the parent of a kid with disabilities.
我写的那篇《波士顿环球报》的文章，有点像是承认了我自己的无知，就像我在这场采访中所做的一样，它有点带领你经历这样一段心路历程：我成了一个残疾儿童的家长。

00:32:45.609 --> 00:32:54.650
And I was in Hollywood, and I was trying to figure out how to make the disability-parenthood part of my life fit, the Hollywood part of my life.
当时我在好莱坞，我试图弄清楚如何将我生活中“残疾父母”的那一部分与我生活中“好莱坞”的那一部分融合起来。

00:32:54.650 --> 00:32:57.329
And I wrote about the ways that it didn't fit, right?
我写下了它们无法融合的那些方面，对吧？

00:32:57.329 --> 00:33:03.049
I wrote about ways that I felt very lonely and unseen in Hollywood, and I didn't know how to talk about it myself.
我写下了我在好莱坞感到非常孤独、无人理解的那些时刻，而且我自己也不知道该如何谈论这件事。

00:33:03.049 --> 00:33:15.609
And then retrospectively, I think about, If I could go back in time, I would have put people, I would have cast roles on how much mother, more often with people with disabilities, I would have made that visible on my show.
然后回过头来想，如果我能时光倒流，我会在《老爸老妈浪漫史》中更频繁地安排残疾演员来出演角色，我会让这种可见性出现在我的节目中。

00:33:15.609 --> 00:33:21.609
I was so young, I was in my 30s, I didn't know how to be the parent of a kid with a disability, right?
我当时太年轻了，才三十多岁，我不知道该怎么做一个残疾孩子的家长，对吧？

00:33:21.609 --> 00:33:37.470
I had no knowledge, and I was trying to run that TV show and raise my son, and I wasn't coming at it with this higher level of awareness, and like realizing I should try to incorporate this into my work and make this visible an emotional palpable reel in my work, I thought they were two separate things.
我毫无经验，我既要制作那档电视节目，又要抚养我的儿子，我并没有带着这种更高层次的觉悟去看待问题，也没有意识到我应该试着把这一点融入到我的工作中，让它在我的工作中变得可见、充满情感、触手可及且真实。我当时认为这两者是完全分开的两码事。

00:33:37.470 --> 00:33:44.190
And now I realize that's not true, and I'm trying to make art and writing about this now, about this topic.
而现在我意识到事实并非如此，我现在正尝试围绕这个主题创作艺术作品和文学。

00:33:44.190 --> 00:33:46.269
But when I was doing how much mother, I didn't know how.
但当年我在做《老爸老妈浪漫史》时，我不知道该怎么做。

00:33:46.269 --> 00:33:55.630
And so that Boston Globe essay, I sort of call myself out a little bit and say, I wish I could go back in time and hire writers with disabilities and cast roles.
所以在那篇《波士顿环球报》的文章里，我算是稍微反思了一下自己，说我希望自己能时光倒流，去雇佣有残疾的编剧并安排残疾演员。

00:33:55.630 --> 00:33:58.549
But I didn't, I just, I was not doing 3D thinking.
但我没有，我只是——我当时并没有进行立体思维。

00:33:58.549 --> 00:34:03.549
I was so day to day, just trying to like be there from my son and run this TV show.
我每天疲于应付，只是想为我的儿子守候在那里，并运作好这档电视节目。

00:34:03.549 --> 00:34:08.349
And it was, I'll admit, it was like all too hard for me to think on that higher level.
而且，我必须承认，要在那种更高的高度上去思考，对我来说实在太难了。

00:34:08.349 --> 00:34:15.829
But now I'm trying more to think on that higher level and say, how can you put things out there in the world that humanize this?
但现在，我正在更多地努力进行那种更高维度的思考，并思考：你究竟该如何在世间呈现出能让这一切人性化的作品呢？

00:34:15.829 --> 00:34:21.710
How can you use whatever your little talents or skill sets are, whatever your ability to communicate is, like how do we do that?
你如何利用自己微小的才华或技能组合，以及你的沟通能力——我们到底该怎么做？

00:34:21.710 --> 00:34:23.110
How do we make it human?
我们该如何让它充满人性？

00:34:23.110 --> 00:34:25.670
How do we make it a felt experience for people?
我们该如何让它成为人们能够真切感受到的体验？

00:34:25.670 --> 00:34:42.309
Because if it stays abstract, people can insult and diminish and swat away things in the abstract that when they are face to face with it and it feels real, even if it's a novel, even if it's fictional, but if it feels real and you feel like real people, you can't dismiss it and disparage it so easily.
因为如果它停留在抽象层面，人们就可以在抽象中侮辱、贬低和挥之不去那些东西；但当他们直面它，并且感觉它是真实存在的时候——哪怕它是虚构的、哪怕是小说，但只要它感觉真实，让你觉得那就是真人，你就无法那么轻易地否定和诋毁它。

00:34:42.309 --> 00:34:43.909
You have to see the whole person.
你必须看到完整的人。

00:34:43.909 --> 00:34:44.989
You have to see the whole person.
你必须看到完整的人。

00:34:44.989 --> 00:34:49.909
And I want to ask you Ray, what did it feel like to you getting that word to throw in it?
我想问问你，雷，听到那个词被扔向你时，你是什么感觉？

00:34:49.909 --> 00:35:03.550
What did that feel like, did that feel like, did you know, I don't know when you got your diagnosis or when you, to what extent you felt you were hiding and then people would say something to you, but it must have been like really shocking and horrible to have someone say something like that to you.
那是什么感觉？那感觉像是——你知道的，我不知道你是什么时候确诊的，或者在某种程度上你觉得自己一直在隐瞒，然后别人会对你说些什么，但当有人对你说出那样的话时，一定是非常震惊和可怕的。

00:35:03.550 --> 00:35:05.670
It felt like getting caught.
感觉像是被当场抓获。

00:35:05.670 --> 00:35:07.340
It felt like getting caught.
感觉像是被当场抓获。

00:35:07.340 --> 00:35:18.030
And you would just, but you also kind of do the thing, you know, like, and I think everybody at a young age does this when somebody says something that hurts you, right?
你会——但你也会做出那种反应，你知道的，就像……我觉得每个人在年轻时都会这样，当有人说出伤害你的话时，对吧？

00:35:18.030 --> 00:35:23.789
You just kind of like, oh, this kind of like shambling, brush it off, wiggle away from it.
你就会有点像，哦，这种感觉就像是拖着步子、抖落掉、然后扭动着想从里面逃开。

00:35:23.789 --> 00:35:32.139
But for me, because I knew that I wasn't, I've used this phrase a lot on this, and I keep saying that because I keep using it.
但对我来说，因为我知道我不是——我在这节目里经常用这个短语，我一直重复是因为我一直在用它。

00:35:32.139 --> 00:35:34.260
But I knew that I was not like the others.
但我知道我跟其他人不一样。

00:35:34.260 --> 00:35:35.860
I was one of these things that was not like the others.
我是那种“与众不同的事物”之一。

00:35:35.860 --> 00:35:37.579
I have ADHD, I have this calculator.
我有注意力缺陷多动障碍（ADHD），我脑子里有个计算器。

00:35:37.579 --> 00:35:38.980
As a kid, I also had this graphia.
小时候，我还患有书写困难症。

00:35:38.980 --> 00:35:41.780
I couldn't write, my spatial awareness is not great.
我不会写字，我的空间意识不太好。

00:35:43.139 --> 00:35:45.380
But I could always cover.
但我总能伪装过去。

00:35:45.380 --> 00:35:51.940
And so when somebody would say like, what are you, I would think like, oh my God, I didn't do it well enough.
所以当有人说“你算个什么东西”时，我会想：“哦，天哪，我伪装得还不够好。”

00:35:51.940 --> 00:35:58.099
I would freeze and feel like I had been seen in a way that was not the way I wanted to be.
我会僵住，觉得自己以一种我并不希望的方式被看透了。

00:35:58.099 --> 00:35:58.613
noticed.
被注意到了。

00:35:58.613 --> 00:36:03.558
Did you literally have those diagnoses or those categories or labels upon it?
你当时真的有这些诊断、这些类别或者说标签贴在身上吗？

00:36:03.558 --> 00:36:04.842
I don't know, right?
我不知道，对吧？

00:36:04.842 --> 00:36:31.619
You knew that you had challenges, maybe your parents knew, but you felt like you're just going to have to fit in because I'm assuming for contemporary ages that there was not much support or much recognition or much conversation on the levels that you would have needed it or that you would hopefully get today if you were a teenager today, but I, you know, assuming you felt very lonely and like, I felt very lonely.
你知道自己有挑战，也许你父母知道，但你觉得你只能去适应，因为我猜在那个年代，并没有太多的支持、认可，也没有那种你所需要的深层对话——如果你今天是个十几岁的孩子，你或许能得到这些。但我猜你当时一定感到非常孤独，就像——我当时觉得非常孤独。

00:36:31.619 --> 00:36:32.119
Yes.
是的。

00:36:32.119 --> 00:36:34.321
And also, I mean, I was lucky enough.
而且，我的意思是，我很幸运。

00:36:34.321 --> 00:36:40.031
I have enough privilege to have been diagnosed at 21, which for a woman is frankly pretty early.
我有着足够的特权，在21岁时就被确诊了，对于女性来说，坦白讲这算相当早了。

00:36:40.031 --> 00:36:41.935
And, but I failed out of school.
但是，我学业失败了。

00:36:41.935 --> 00:36:45.445
I mean, I like crashed and burned in every conceivable way.
我是说，我在各个能想到的方面都彻底崩溃和失败了。

00:36:45.445 --> 00:36:57.462
And, you know, if you were to look at like, what are the negative consequences of having learning disabilities on paper and being on diagnosed, like you could basically read my story up to about age 21.
你知道的，如果你去看看“未确诊的学习障碍在纸面上会带来什么负面后果”，你基本上就可以直接读我的故事，一直读到我21岁左右。

00:36:57.199 --> 00:36:58.186
Yeah, yeah, yeah.
对，对，对。

00:36:58.186 --> 00:37:15.365
But that word for me and one of the things that I struggle with even in this conversation is when I'm like, I'm part of the disability community, because I was able to pass, because it wasn't something that I had to wear all the time, I feel sometimes like I'm like an imposter within that space.
但对我来说那个词——即使在这场对话中我也在挣扎的事情之一是，当我想要说“我是残障群体的一部分”时，因为我能够伪装过去，因为这不是一件我必须时刻穿在身上的外衣，我有时觉得自己在这个空间里像个冒名顶替者。

00:37:15.365 --> 00:37:17.223
Like, you know, I could hide it.
就像，你知道的，我能把它藏起来。

00:37:17.223 --> 00:37:22.968
So do I really get to call myself disabled in this, like, to me, that feels like an inclusive word.
所以我真的有资格在这时称自己为残障人士吗？对我来说，这似乎是一个具有包容性的词。

00:37:22.968 --> 00:37:27.379
I'm part of this wonderful community that is like a privilege to be part of.
我是这个美妙社群的一部分，能成为其中一员是一种特权。

00:37:27.379 --> 00:37:33.589
But I worry sometimes that when I use it, I'm like, my friend used to call for other things, fake the funk.
但我有时会担心，当我使用它时，就像我朋友以前对其他事情的称呼那样——“假装时髦”（fake the funk）。

00:37:33.589 --> 00:37:55.179
Like, I'm, you know, like, so it was, it's a tricky word in that way of if you have an invisible disability, you both relate to it and experience it, but in a way that I think also when we hear from people with Down syndrome, where we hear from people who have clear disabilities that are like physically obvious, most of them say they've never had that word said to them.
就像我是——你知道的，所以这是一个很棘手的词，如果你有隐形残障，你既能与它产生共鸣并经历它，但另一方面，我想当我们听到唐氏综合征患者的心声，或者听到那些有着肉眼可见的明显残障的人的心声时，他们大多数人都会说，从没有人对他们说过那个词。

00:37:55.179 --> 00:37:56.019
Yeah.
对。

00:37:56.019 --> 00:38:10.820
Because what you're talking about, people don't, most people don't think of themselves as the kind of person who would show up and call someone who had a visible disability, the R word, but they're perfectly happy to use it online or you would put somebody who they don't realize is in the club.
因为你所谈论的这些，人们不会——大多数人不会认为自己是那种会跑去对一个有着明显残障的人说那个“R”打头的词的人，但他们却非常乐意在网上使用它，或者对一个他们没有意识到其实也是“俱乐部成员”的人使用它。

00:38:10.820 --> 00:38:12.170
Yes.
是的。

00:38:12.170 --> 00:38:15.090
Oh man, that is also fascinating.
天哪，这也太迷人了。

00:38:15.090 --> 00:38:18.849
you saying you felt caught in that moment is just like so heartbreaking.
你说你在那一刻觉得自己被抓包了，真的太让人心碎了。

00:38:21.969 --> 00:38:30.374
And in terms of like you're at your identity, I mean to me, I'm the parent of a child with a disability and I write about this sometimes.
至于你的身份认同，我是说，对我而言，我是一个残障儿童的家长，我有时也会写这方面的文章。

00:38:30.374 --> 00:38:32.214
I sometimes feel that way too.
我有时也会有那种感觉。

00:38:32.214 --> 00:38:34.913
Do I have a right to write about this topic?
我有权写这个话题吗？

00:38:34.913 --> 00:38:37.980
I'm only the parent of somebody with a disability.
我只是一个残障人士的家长。

00:38:37.980 --> 00:38:41.477
It's somebody who I love more than anything in the world.
虽然他是我世上最爱的人。

00:38:41.477 --> 00:39:05.401
But I think we all have that imposter syndrome of what authority or authenticity to have talked about these things, but to me, for what it's worth, you're doing a beautiful thing by talking about all of this and your journey and you are part of that community and you're using your talents and your voice to talk about it and that makes you a part of the community and you already were too.
但我想我们都会有那种“冒名顶替综合征”，觉得自己有什么权威或真实性去谈论这些事情呢？不过对我来说，无论它有多大价值，你在谈论所有这一切以及你的心路历程时，你正在做一件美丽的事，你就是那个社群的一部分，你正在用你的才华和声音来谈论它，这让你成为了社群的一部分，而且你本来就是。

00:39:05.401 --> 00:39:08.529
So it's like, you should not overthink that, right?
所以这就好比，你不应该过度思考这一点，对吧？

00:39:08.529 --> 00:39:11.229
We all need each other in this conversation.
在这场对话中，我们都需要彼此。

00:39:11.229 --> 00:39:20.860
And we are up against a culture that views, people with disabilities, and really ignorant distance of ways sometimes, and in ways that are invisible to them.
而我们所对抗的文化，是以一种极其无知和疏离的方式来看待残障人士的，而且这种方式对他们自己而言是隐形的。

00:39:20.860 --> 00:39:24.260
I think the argument for the R word is sometimes like, it's just a funny word.
我认为人们为那个“R”打头的词辩解时有时会说：“这只是个好玩的词。我说这话时并没有想到残障人士。”

00:39:24.260 --> 00:39:27.739
I'm not thinking of a person with a disability when I say that.
问题是，它确实伤害了残障人士的感情。

00:39:27.739 --> 00:39:31.900
The problem is, it does hurt the feelings of people with disabilities.
你不会在公共场合到处宣扬某个针对种族的侮辱性词汇，因为你知道会有人对此极为不满，并质问你：“你刚才为什么说那个词？”

00:39:31.900 --> 00:39:40.179
You're not running around saying a racial epithet in public, because you know somebody's going to take huge issue with it and say, why did you just say that word?
所以，当这个社群说这伤害了他们——他们的确这么说了——那么“这只是一个被抽象化、现在成了一个有趣的词、反正也没人在乎”这种论调，根本就是无稽之谈，人们是在乎的。

00:39:40.179 --> 00:39:52.799
And so with that community says it hurts them, which they do say that, then the argument that it's just this word that's been abstracted and now it's just a funny word and nobody cares, people care.
你猜怎么着？

00:39:52.799 --> 00:39:53.501
Guess what?
人们是在乎的。

00:39:53.501 --> 00:39:54.265
People care.
人们确实在乎。

00:39:54.265 --> 00:39:55.222
People do care.
这确实很重要。

00:39:55.222 --> 00:39:56.178
It does matter.
所以这种论点根本站不住脚，我是这么认为的。

00:39:56.178 --> 00:39:58.791
So that argument holds no water, I think.
对。

00:39:58.791 --> 00:39:59.291
Yeah.
我能问你个问题吗？因为你刚才对我都那么善良和肯定，我也想稍微回应你一下。

00:39:59.291 --> 00:40:06.238
Can I ask you, because you did, we're so kind and validating just then, and I want to return it a little bit.
作为一位家长，你曾说过，你希望自己在做节目、刚生下埃利奥特（Elliot）、正努力想把这两件事兼顾好的时候，能知道更多就好了。

00:40:06.238 --> 00:40:17.583
As a parent, you said, I wish I'd known more when you were doing the show and you just had Elliot and you were trying to figure out how you could bring those two things together.
对于任何人来说，经历那种状态都是一种极其强烈、高压的体验，哪怕你的孩子没有任何状况。

00:40:17.583 --> 00:40:24.531
That is a wildly intense experience to go through for anyone, even if there's nothing going on with your kid.
就算你的工作再简单，要抚养一个婴儿、一个不断长大的孩子，还要兼顾工作，这本身就已经很不容易了。

00:40:24.531 --> 00:40:30.458
Even if your job is a simple job, it's a lot to have a baby and then growing child and a job.
我只是想对这一点表示肯定。

00:40:30.458 --> 00:40:32.307
I just want to validate that.
我觉得你不需要去想“天哪，我希望我当时能做得更好”，因为虽然我们作为父母、作为凡人，总是会忍不住这么想、也确实一直在这么做。但我真的很想问，作为现在的家长，你拥有了所有的这些视角，埃利奥特已经18岁了，对于他的未来，你有什么感到兴奋的事吗？

00:40:32.307 --> 00:40:48.560
I don't think you need to be like, God, I wish I had been better because although we always and always do, as parents as people, but I do want to ask, as a parent now, you have all this perspective, and Elliot's 18, and what are you excited about for him?
反过来说，作为这个即将独自走向世界的孩子的家长，有什么事让你夜不能寐？

00:40:48.560 --> 00:40:56.081
And on the flip side of that, what's keeping you up at night as the parent of a child who's going out into this world?
是的，问得好。

00:40:55.900 --> 00:40:57.009
Yeah, great question.
谢谢你的这番话。

00:40:57.009 --> 00:40:58.435
Thank you for that comment.
我其实还是会——但这真的是一句很暖心的评价。

00:40:58.435 --> 00:41:00.759
I do still, but that's a port of of comment.
我是说，我还是会看——我想在这点上补充一件事，因为我确实还是会回首过去。

00:41:00.759 --> 00:41:05.671
I mean, I do still look, I want to say one thing on that, because I do still look back on it.
我会尽量不那么苛责自己。

00:41:05.671 --> 00:41:07.784
I'll try not to beat myself up too much.
但我很遗憾，当时我拥有某种平台，我本可以利用它来谈论更多这方面的事情。

00:41:07.784 --> 00:41:13.330
But I regret that I had a certain kind of platform that I probably could have used to talk about it more.
你想听实话吗，对吧？

00:41:13.330 --> 00:41:15.284
Do you want to know the truth, right?
我觉得我当时其实有点在伪装，对吧？

00:41:15.284 --> 00:41:17.502
I think I was masking a little bit, right?
我当时的心态是，嗯，走着瞧吧。

00:41:17.502 --> 00:41:19.668
I was like, well, we'll see what happens.
你知道的，我并没有完全去拥抱他的状况——他患有一种罕见的综合征。

00:41:19.668 --> 00:41:23.260
You know, I wasn't like fully embracing his, he has a rare syndrome.
我不知道未来具体会走向何方。

00:41:23.260 --> 00:41:25.715
I don't know exactly which way it's going to go.
当时确实存在某种程度的否认，或者说没有完全接受正在发生的事情，或者说大脑无法进行足够的计算，以至于不知道该如何去书写它或谈论它。

00:41:25.715 --> 00:41:34.360
There was some level of like denial or not fully embracing what was happening or not being able to compute it well enough to know how to write about it or talk about it.
我只是在私生活中慢慢地和别人分享这件事，但这确实是一条未知的道路，一种罕见的基因综合征。

00:41:34.360 --> 00:41:40.293
I was sort of slowly sharing it in my personal life with people, but it is an unknown road, a rare genetic syndrome.
世界上患有雅各布综合征（Jacob's syndrome）的人其实并不多。

00:41:40.293 --> 00:41:43.516
There aren't that many people at Jacob's syndrome in the world.
即便是在这个群体内部，也有着非常多不同的表现形式。

00:41:43.516 --> 00:41:47.659
And even within that population, there's a lot of different manifestations of it.
50%的雅各布综合征患者都有某种心脏问题。

00:41:47.659 --> 00:41:51.036
50% of people at Jacob's syndrome have some sort of heart problem.
另外50%则没有。

00:41:51.036 --> 00:41:52.059
The other 50% don't.
但这证明了人类基因组那复杂的鸡尾酒效应：为什么我基本上缺失了第11号染色体的相同片段，只是多一点或少一些，为什么有些人的心脏没有正确发育，而另一些人的却可以？这真是令人难以置信的着迷。

00:41:52.059 --> 00:42:07.699
But that is a testament to the complicated cocktail of the human genome, why I'm missing basically the same parts of the 11th chromosome, they're just more or less, do some people's hearts not sort of form correctly, but others do, it's sort of incredibly fascinating.
这就是为什么会有一位了不起的医生在研究雅各布综合征，他就是保罗·格罗斯菲尔德医生（Dr. Paul Grossfeld），他是那个社群的一盏明灯，绝大多数已发表的医学文献和研究都归功于他。

00:42:07.699 --> 00:42:18.579
That's why there's this amazing doctor that studies Jacob Simpson of Dr. Paul Grossfeld that is like the beacon of light to that community and like he's the reason for so much of the published medical literature, his research.
我做了很多筹款活动来支持他的研究，他太棒了。但在当时，我并不知道，我不知道这到底会变成什么样，对吧？我没有谈论太多，我想那是我当时的一种方式，有点像是在雷达底下航行——我不想让别人以某种特定方式来看待我或我的儿子，就好像在逃避它，或者觉得有某种隐瞒它的需要一样。我的内心有某种抗拒，抗拒把它分享给我的核心圈子——除了极少数几个人、《老爸老妈浪漫史》剧组和我的朋友们之外。

00:42:18.579 --> 00:42:51.380
I do a lot of fundraising to support his research and he's amazing but at that time I didn't know I didn't know exactly what it was going to be right and I didn't talk about it that much and I think that was my way of being a little bit like I'm just sailing under the radar I don't want people to see me or see my son this certain way like as if there's some kind of hiding from it or some kind of need to hide from it there was something resisting it in my mind like resisting sharing it wider than my, than the small handful of people and how I met your mother and my friends.
我想这——我不知道，这让我想起了那种感觉，就像你说的“被抓包”的时刻，或者说，那种你必须去接受一件艰难之事的时刻，对吧？

00:42:51.380 --> 00:43:04.485
And I think that's, I don't know, it just, it reminded me of that feeling like when you said you got caught or something, like the moment where like the, the, you have to sort of embrace this hard thing, right?
一件很难向世界启齿、让你感到脆弱的事情，但我想你会慢慢成长并适应那个角色。

00:43:04.485 --> 00:43:11.662
That's hard to share with the world and you feel vulnerable doing it, but you sort of grow into that role, I guess.
希望如此吧，我想我正在努力这么做，但——是的，没错。

00:43:11.662 --> 00:43:18.340
Hopefully, I guess I'm trying to, but yeah, Yeah, so that's me, when Ellie gets a baby, here I am, he's 18.
所以，埃利奥特曾经是个小婴儿，而现在他18岁了。

00:43:18.340 --> 00:43:23.619
In between, I feel like I've learned so much, so much.
在这期间，我觉得自己学到了太多太多。

00:43:23.619 --> 00:43:25.260
He's amazing.
他太棒了。

00:43:25.260 --> 00:43:29.980
He has taught me everything important to know.
他教会了我所有真正重要的事情。

00:43:29.980 --> 00:43:32.699
He is the most musical person I've ever met.
他是我见过的最有音乐天赋的人。

00:43:32.699 --> 00:43:35.780
He is this wonderfully musical soul.
他有着这样一个美妙的音乐灵魂。

00:43:35.780 --> 00:43:46.579
So imagine that this is a guy who he has a lot of fine and gross motor scoldaways and and spatial problems with spatial relationships and save their safety issues around that.
所以想象一下，这是一个在精细运动技能、粗大运动技能以及空间关系上有很多障碍、并且周围存在安全隐患的家伙。

00:43:46.579 --> 00:43:49.340
This is a guy who, he can't button a button.
这是一个连扣子都扣不上的家伙。

00:43:50.260 --> 00:43:55.019
He can't really do handwriting, but he can play Led Zeppelin on the drums.
他其实不会写字，但他可以用架子鼓打齐柏林飞艇乐队的歌。

00:43:55.019 --> 00:43:56.860
He can play Radiohead on the drums.
他可以用架子鼓打电台司令乐队的歌。

00:43:56.860 --> 00:43:58.380
He can play the Pixies on the drums.
他可以用架子鼓打小妖精乐队的歌。

00:43:58.380 --> 00:44:00.139
Basically, I've indoctrinated him to think it's 1996.
基本上，我给他灌输的思想就是现在还是1996年。

00:44:00.139 --> 00:44:02.500
I was gonna say that's a pretty good music choice.
我本想说这音乐品味真挺不错的。

00:44:02.500 --> 00:44:03.340
It's across the board.
各种风格都有。

00:44:03.340 --> 00:44:04.179
Jam is his biggest one.
他最喜欢的还是杰姆乐队。

00:44:04.179 --> 00:44:05.619
I should say Pearl Jam.
我应该说是珍珠果酱乐队。

00:44:05.619 --> 00:44:08.340
He's basically like a dad rocker at the age 18.
他基本上就像个18岁的摇滚老爹。

00:44:08.340 --> 00:44:12.179
He's like, Can you do the Addy Better voice to like bag it up then?
他会说：“那你能不能用那种‘老爸老妈浪漫史’的语气把东西装起来？”

00:44:12.179 --> 00:44:13.380
Not in public, I can't.
在公共场合不行，我可做不到。

00:44:13.380 --> 00:44:19.179
If you walk by my house at the wrong moment, you might hear it and go, what is happening inside there?
如果你在不合时宜的时候路过我家，你可能会听到然后心想：里面到底在发生什么？

00:44:19.179 --> 00:44:20.500
What's your address?
你家地址是多少？

00:44:20.500 --> 00:44:24.500
But he, so I taught him, I'm a drummer.
但他……我教过他，我是一名鼓手。

00:44:24.500 --> 00:44:25.659
I taught him drums.
我教过他打鼓。

00:44:25.659 --> 00:44:27.980
He got so good at the drums, I got kicked off the drums.
他打鼓打得太好了，结果把我给比下去了。

00:44:27.980 --> 00:44:31.260
And now I just play guitar and sing and pretend to be Addy Better at times.
现在我只能弹吉他、唱歌，偶尔假装自己是老爸老妈浪漫史里的角色。

00:44:31.260 --> 00:44:35.179
And we probably play like 50, 70 songs together.
我们俩大概一起弹奏了50到70首歌。

00:44:35.179 --> 00:44:36.579
We have like this catalog.
我们有这样一个曲库。

00:44:36.579 --> 00:44:39.139
And so he, this is a guy drumming his heart.
所以他，这是一个全身心投入打鼓的小伙子。

00:44:39.139 --> 00:44:41.860
You have four different limbs doing four different things.
你有四个不同的肢体在做四件不同的事情。

00:44:41.860 --> 00:44:43.260
Yeah, I can't do it at all.
对，我完全做不到。

00:44:43.260 --> 00:44:46.139
He can do that, but he can't button a button.
他能做到，但他却不会系纽扣。

00:44:47.139 --> 00:44:49.940
Zippering Zipper was in the last couple of years he got that.
拉拉链——拉拉链是这两年才学会的。

00:44:49.940 --> 00:45:03.780
I mean, this is all to say one wonderful word, I think, to describe one, and not wonderful, an accurate word to describe him when people say, well, what word would a, the atypical learner idea is fairly applicable to him.
我的意思是，这一切都可以用一个美妙的词来概括——我觉得，用来形容他，而且不仅仅是美妙，是一个准确的词。当人们说，好吧，会用什么词呢？“非典型学习者”这个概念相当适合他。

00:45:03.780 --> 00:45:08.019
He just has these different challenges and these different huge strengths.
他只是有着这些不同的挑战和这些巨大的优势。

00:45:08.019 --> 00:45:11.739
And it is so interesting to watch that journey.
看着这段旅程真的非常有趣。

00:45:12.940 --> 00:45:19.380
He's funny, he's sweet, he's musical, and he just played drums in the pit band for his school musical.
他很幽默，很可爱，很有音乐天赋，他刚刚在学校音乐剧的乐队里担任鼓手。

00:45:19.380 --> 00:45:21.500
And I'm basically in a band with my son.
我基本上是和我儿子组了个乐队。

00:45:21.500 --> 00:45:27.739
And we do this, we do a high-metromother based fundraiser concert for that amazing doctor I just talked about, who's research.
我们在为我刚才提到的那位了不起的医生的研究做一个以“老爸老妈浪漫史”为主题的筹款音乐会。

00:45:27.739 --> 00:45:29.059
So we've done it three times now.
所以我们现在已经办过三次了。

00:45:29.059 --> 00:45:31.059
If anyone's in New York, please follow me on social media.
如果有人在纽约，请在社交媒体上关注我。

00:45:31.059 --> 00:45:31.980
That's so cool.
太酷了。

00:45:31.980 --> 00:46:08.063
And check this out, because we do it once a year, and we have actors from the cast of How Metromother come, we have musicians who's music we use how much mother at this past one we had Josh Radner and Wolf Forte and the lead singer of Nada Serf and all an amazing lineup of people of all those stars and celebrities Elliot was the star of the night because I got him out and he played drums on four songs with us in front of 650 people we packed the Bowery Ballroom but that if you're asking where are what are those moments of joy what are those moments I wish I could go back in time how much mother was a very time jumpy show where we jump to flashback, flash forward.
来看看这个，因为我们每年办一次，我们会请来《老爸老妈浪漫史》剧组的演员，还有那些我们用过他们音乐的音乐人。在最近的一次活动中，我们请来了乔什·拉德纳、威尔·福特，还有纳达·瑟夫乐队的主唱，以及所有这些明星和名人组成的惊人阵容。但当晚真正的明星是埃利奥特，因为我把他叫上了台，在650人面前和我们一起用四首歌打了架子鼓，我们挤满了包厘舞厅。如果你在问，什么是那些快乐的时刻？什么是那些我希望能够时光倒流的时刻？《老爸老妈浪漫史》是一部经常跳跃时间的剧，我们在其中会闪回到过去，闪现到未来。

00:46:08.063 --> 00:46:13.011
If I could flash back to when my son was just born and I thought, how am I going to do this?
如果我能闪回到我刚生下儿子的时候，当时我想，我该怎么面对这一切？

00:46:13.011 --> 00:46:14.517
How are we going to do this?
我们该怎么面对这一切？

00:46:14.517 --> 00:46:16.237
How is my wife going to do this?
我妻子该怎么面对这一切？

00:46:16.237 --> 00:46:17.958
How are we all going to do this?
我们所有人该怎么面对这一切？

00:46:17.958 --> 00:46:38.339
If I could have had a crystal ball and gone back now, I'm 50, basically, and I go back with a crystal ball and I just get to give like 30 year old Craig or whatever, 32 year old Craig, just one glimpse in a crystal ball, it might be Elliott playing drums in front of 650 people at Valerie Ballroom with with a full band, including professional musicians, and honestly killing it.
如果我能有一个水晶球然后回到现在——我基本上50岁了——我带着水晶球回去，让我给30岁的克雷格，或者不管哪个32岁的克雷格，通过水晶球瞥一眼未来，那可能就是埃利奥特在包厘舞厅的650人面前打鼓，和包括专业乐手在内的完整乐队一起，老实说，简直燃炸了。

00:46:38.340 --> 00:46:39.539
Honestly, nailing it.
说实话，表现完美。

00:46:39.539 --> 00:46:41.219
He is so rock steady.
他的节奏感稳如磐石。

00:46:41.219 --> 00:46:42.300
He was so great.
他表现得太棒了。

00:46:42.300 --> 00:46:43.760
I was up on guitar singing those songs.
我当时在台上弹吉他唱那些歌。

00:46:43.760 --> 00:46:46.860
We did a few comedy songs from how much mother that Carter and I wrote.
我们唱了几首卡特和我为《老爸老妈浪漫史》创作的搞笑歌曲。

00:46:46.860 --> 00:46:50.179
And I messed up the lyrics at one point, and he was rock steady.
结果我在某个地方记错词了，而他的节奏依然稳如磐石。

00:46:50.179 --> 00:46:51.780
I totally blanked on some lyrics.
我脑子里有那么一秒钟完全一片空白。

00:46:51.780 --> 00:46:58.900
I had to like, bam, for a second, and he was rock steady, and just so, I was so proud of him.
我得卡壳一下下，但他稳如磐石，而且真的……我为他感到无比骄傲。

00:46:58.900 --> 00:47:03.699
And just one of those things you're like, I just wish I could have seen that moment.
这就是那种你会觉得“我多么希望自己能亲眼见证那一刻”的事情之一。

00:47:03.699 --> 00:47:21.179
And that could be any number of moments for any number of people, but those gifts you can't imagine when you're catastrophizing and your worst-case scenario and you're reading the Google page about the genetic syndrome and reading every worst thing and going, all of those are going to be in my sense.
对任何人来说，这都可以是无数时刻中的任何一个，但当你陷入灾难化思维、想着最坏的情况、在谷歌上搜索基因综合症并阅读所有最糟糕的事情，心想“所有这些都会发生在我儿子身上”时，你是无法想象这些天赋的。

00:47:21.179 --> 00:47:30.230
And then what you realize, again, coming back to humanizing people, then you look at the human being that is your child and you realize this is a human being.
然后你意识到——再次回到将人视作有血有肉的个体——你看着身为你的孩子的这个人，你意识到这是一个活生生的人。

00:47:30.230 --> 00:47:31.429
There's a lot of factors here.
这里面有很多因素。

00:47:31.429 --> 00:47:35.670
Yes, he's missing a little piece of one of his 11th chromosomes, but he's him.
没错，他的第11号染色体少了一小段，但他就是他。

00:47:35.670 --> 00:47:40.030
He's not reducible to WebMD page of symptoms.
他不能被简化为网络医学百科全书上的症状页面。

00:47:40.030 --> 00:47:41.630
He's not reducible to the R word.
他不能被简化为那个带有歧视色彩的词。

00:47:41.630 --> 00:47:48.809
He's not reducible to anything because he is a beautiful human being, and he's sorry.
他不能被简化为任何东西，因为他是一个美丽的人，而且抱歉。

00:47:48.809 --> 00:47:54.170
He's going to be more than anybody could ever imagine.
他的成就将超越任何人所能想象的极限。

00:47:54.170 --> 00:47:58.170
He's going to be himself and he cannot be reduced to any one thing.
他将做他自己，他绝不能被简化为单一的某一方面。

00:47:58.170 --> 00:48:07.250
But I think that that's the gift of it, the challenge of it is wanting the world to see him that way.
但我觉得这既是天赋，也是挑战，挑战在于希望世界能以这种眼光看待他。

00:48:07.250 --> 00:48:24.134
And now that he's 18, I want him to go out in the world and be seen with that sort of robust, generous, open-minded approach rather than something reductive, and that how his next few years will go and what his education will be and how that will all work is that stuff keeps me up at night.
既然他已经18岁了，我希望他能走向世界，带着那种全面、慷慨、思想开放的态度被人看待，而不是某种单一刻板的眼光。他接下来的几年会怎么过，他的教育会怎样，这一切将如何运作——这些事情让我夜不能寐。

00:48:24.134 --> 00:48:26.222
And it's a lot of problems to solve.
有太多问题需要解决。

00:48:26.222 --> 00:48:32.315
And like I said at the beginning of the conversation, it's, it's range, how much it is in the wilderness.
就像我在对话开始时说的，这范围很广，很大程度上就像是在荒野中摸索。

00:48:32.315 --> 00:48:42.990
And it's each person you talk to who's gone through it as the parents who've gone through it, trying to devise sort of next steps for their child with collaboratively with their child.
你交谈的每一个人，那些经历过这一切的父母，都在试图与他们的孩子合作，为孩子规划下一步。

00:48:42.990 --> 00:48:44.905
Everyone is doing an improv game.
每个人都在玩一场即兴游戏。

00:48:44.905 --> 00:48:50.185
There's just everyone's like, well, we did this for a couple years and we figured this out.
大家都在说，好吧，我们这样搞了几年，然后我们摸索出了这个。

00:48:50.185 --> 00:48:56.741
And we found this thing where it's this company that has people who, who have disabilities and they welcome them.
我们找到了这样一家公司，里面有残障人士，他们很欢迎这些人。

00:48:56.741 --> 00:49:02.369
And And then he did have a couple of, it's all like this, this in constant game of improvisation.
然后他确实经历了几次……这一切就像是一场永无止境的即兴游戏。

00:49:02.369 --> 00:49:03.369
Yeah.
对。

00:49:03.369 --> 00:49:04.369
Yeah.
对。

00:49:04.369 --> 00:49:05.369
Sorry.
抱歉。

00:49:05.369 --> 00:49:06.369
Sorry.
抱歉。

00:49:06.369 --> 00:49:07.369
Got emotional there.
刚才情绪有点激动。

00:49:07.369 --> 00:49:08.369
It's funny.
很有趣。

00:49:08.369 --> 00:49:10.610
You can tell there's just so much in this conversation.
你能感觉到这场对话里包含了太多东西。

00:49:10.610 --> 00:49:11.610
There's a lot, right?
有很多对吧？

00:49:11.610 --> 00:49:12.610
There's a lot.
有很多。

00:49:12.610 --> 00:49:22.849
We're looking at it a lot and all of this is in my book and this is like, I'm trying to channel all of those fears and keep you up at night and find out how can I make this funny?
我们看到了很多，而所有这些都在我的书中，这就好像，我试图引导所有这些让你夜不能寐的恐惧，然后想想：我怎么才能把它写得有趣？

00:49:22.849 --> 00:49:23.849
How can I make this human?
我怎么才能把它写得有人情味？

00:49:23.849 --> 00:49:27.329
How can I write about this in a way that that people will relate to?
我怎么才能以一种能让人们产生共鸣的方式来写这件事？

00:49:27.329 --> 00:49:29.130
whether or not they're in this experience.
无论他们是否有过这种经历。

00:49:29.130 --> 00:49:35.329
Because I think the experience of being a parent and being in a family, my book is about being a parent and being in a family, it's not, it's not.
因为我认为身为父母和身处一个家庭的经历——我的书是关于身为父母和身处一个家庭的，它不是……

00:49:35.329 --> 00:49:44.090
It is a family with one of the members has a disability, but I think every parent, there's this great quote that I love that like, I'll get wrong now.
它确实是一个家庭，其中有一个成员患有残疾，但我认为每一个父母……我有一句很棒的名言我很喜欢，虽然我现在可能会引用错。

00:49:44.090 --> 00:49:49.929
But parenthood is letting your heart come out of your body and walk around that zone.
但为人父母就是让你的心离开你的身体，去那个地带四处走动。

00:49:49.929 --> 00:49:51.329
Yeah, it's when your heart lives outside.
对，就是当你的心活在外面的世界。

00:49:51.329 --> 00:49:52.170
When your heart lives outside, yeah, yeah.
当你的心活在外面，对，对。

00:49:52.170 --> 00:49:56.210
And it's like, that's parenthood, that's all parenthood.
这就好像，这就是为人父母，这就是所有的为人父母。

00:49:56.210 --> 00:49:58.449
That's not disability-based parenthood.
这不仅仅是基于残障的为人父母。

00:49:58.449 --> 00:49:59.769
That is all parenthood.
这就是所有的为人父母。

00:49:59.769 --> 00:50:00.610
Yes, so accurate.
是的，太准确了。

00:50:00.610 --> 00:50:03.010
And what is the future for your child?
你孩子的未来是什么？

00:50:03.010 --> 00:50:05.210
How can you make the best future for your child?
你怎样才能为你的孩子创造最好的未来？

00:50:05.210 --> 00:50:06.889
That is just universal.
这完全是普遍的。

00:50:06.889 --> 00:50:08.909
All of our parents wanted that for us.
我们所有的父母都对我们抱有这样的期望。

00:50:08.909 --> 00:50:12.409
And we, if and when we become parents, we want that for our children.
如果我们成为了父母，我们也对我们的孩子抱有这样的期望。

00:50:12.409 --> 00:50:14.730
That is universal.
这是普遍存在的。

00:50:16.010 --> 00:50:18.010
And that's what my book's about.
这就是我的书所讲述的内容。

00:50:18.010 --> 00:50:21.409
And that's what keeps me up at night.
这也是让我夜不能寐的原因。

00:50:21.409 --> 00:50:31.710
But it's also, you know, my son has shown me time and again that I don't have to be so afraid and that he can do so much.
但同时，你知道的，我的儿子一次又一次地向我证明，我不必那么害怕，他能做到很多事情。

00:50:31.710 --> 00:50:35.250
And yeah, there's a lot of gifts in this journey.
是的，这段旅程中有很多美好的馈赠。

00:50:35.250 --> 00:50:37.320
There's a lot of gifts in this journey.
这段旅程中有很多美好的馈赠。

00:50:37.320 --> 00:50:40.920
I love that he sounds like just an amazing guy.
我喜欢他听起来就是个了不起的家伙。

00:50:40.920 --> 00:50:42.800
He's a really good guy.
他真的是个非常好的人。

00:50:42.800 --> 00:50:46.519
And I love that you just wanted him to see, I don't know, this is how we all feel, right?
我很喜欢你只是想让他看到，我不知道，这就是我们大家的感受，对吧？

00:50:46.519 --> 00:50:51.280
We want our kids to be seen as whole people just for the wonder that they are.
我们希望我们的孩子能作为一个完整的人被看到，仅仅为了他们身上那种奇妙的美好。

00:50:51.280 --> 00:50:52.719
Yes, yes.
是的，是的。

00:50:52.719 --> 00:50:56.565
And I'll say this one other thing about of passing or masking.
关于隐藏或伪装，我还要说另外一件事。

00:50:56.565 --> 00:51:01.280
In the last couple of, in the last year or so, my son has lost all his hair.
在过去的几年里，在过去一年左右的时间里，我儿子把头发全掉光了。

00:51:01.280 --> 00:51:09.948
It's a very rare symptom of Jacob's syndrome, almost to the point where we know a couple other people with Jacob's syndrome this happened to.
这是雅各布综合征的一个非常罕见的症状，几乎到了我们认识的其他几个患有雅各布综合征的人身上也发生过这种情况的程度。

00:51:09.948 --> 00:51:13.513
And so he's completely bald, his eyebrows are mostly gone.
所以他现在完全秃头了，眉毛也几乎掉光了。

00:51:13.513 --> 00:51:16.095
He looks different than he used to, right?
他看起来跟以前不一样了，对吧？

00:51:16.095 --> 00:51:20.767
And he instantly looks more different than he ever did, if that makes sense.
如果可以这么说的话，他比以往任何时候都显得更加与众不同。

00:51:20.767 --> 00:51:28.083
He's more recognizable instantly as someone that has something medical going on or something disability-based going on.
他一眼就能让人认出是一个身体有状况或有某种残疾的人。

00:51:28.083 --> 00:51:32.202
I think people see him differently than they did a year ago, right?
我觉得人们看他的眼光和一年前不一样了，对吧？

00:51:32.202 --> 00:51:44.805
And it's so interesting that idea of masking or being caught or whatever it is because I miss him having his hair because his hair was beautiful and every now and again he goes, I wish I still had my hair.
隐藏、伪装或者被发现诸如此类的想法真的很有意思，因为我很怀念他有头发的样子，因为他的头发很漂亮，而且他时不时会说：“我真希望我还有头发。”

00:51:44.805 --> 00:51:51.259
Other times he goes, I'm just like the drummer of Radiohead, It feels so like he's bald and he's awesome.
其他时候他又会说：“我简直就像电台司令乐队的鼓手。”他光着头的感觉太棒了。

00:51:51.260 --> 00:51:53.579
And I'm like, yes, he is bald and he's awesome.
我就说：“对啊，他是光头，而且他酷毙了。”

00:51:53.579 --> 00:51:55.500
And it's just really interesting.
这真的很有趣。

00:51:56.860 --> 00:52:01.019
It's interesting now to go through the world with him and people look at him differently than they used to.
现在和他一起融入这个世界很有意思，人们看他的眼光和以前不一样了。

00:52:01.019 --> 00:52:07.619
But also there's, I think it's good because it's like, let me embrace that new piece, right?
但同时，我觉得这也挺好的，因为这就好像是在说：让我去拥抱这个全新的部分吧，对吧？

00:52:07.619 --> 00:52:14.820
Each new piece, each new chapter, each new part of this, you've got to wrap your arms around it and go like this is what it is.
每一个新的部分、每一个新的篇章、这其中的每一个新阶段，你都得张开双臂去拥抱它，然后说，事情就是这样。

00:52:14.820 --> 00:52:19.820
And we will figure out life like this now.
我们现在要这样去面对生活了。

00:52:20.099 --> 00:52:22.539
And it's not really gonna change that much.
而且这其实不会改变太多。

00:52:23.420 --> 00:52:25.260
And maybe it'll make everything more honest.
也许这会让一切变得更真诚。

00:52:25.260 --> 00:52:26.780
Maybe I think everything.
也许我觉得一切都会如此。

00:52:26.780 --> 00:52:31.059
Maybe we'll discover gifts in that that we didn't know about.
也许我们会在其中发现我们以前不知道的天赋。

00:52:31.059 --> 00:52:32.820
So it's just interesting, that idea.
所以这种想法真的很有趣。

00:52:32.820 --> 00:52:42.179
The idea that there should ever be any masking or shaming or hiding or passing is so unfortunate that anybody ever has to feel that way.
任何时候都不应该存在任何伪装、羞耻、隐藏或掩饰的想法，任何人都不应该有这种感觉，这太令人遗憾了。

00:52:42.179 --> 00:52:49.560
I think that circles all the way back to the way we talk about and think about disability in America and the world.
我认为这最终可以追溯到我们在美国乃至全世界谈论和看待残疾的方式。

00:52:49.560 --> 00:53:10.226
What words do we use, the space we make to be either inclusive or exclusive, disinclusive, the disincluding people, diminishing people using derogatory terms, there's no one would feel the need to hide or pass or feel shame if we were in a society that didn't use things like the R word and that instead said, how do we...
我们使用什么词汇，我们为实现包容、排斥、反包容、把人排除在外、贬低他人、使用贬义词而腾出的空间，如果我们身处这样一个社会：人们不会使用诸如“傻子”之类的词，而是会说“我们该如何……”，那么就不会有人觉得有必要隐藏、伪装或感到羞耻……

00:53:10.226 --> 00:53:13.242
How do we include, how do we open up the world?
我们如何包容，我们如何向世界敞开大门？

00:53:13.242 --> 00:53:16.579
How do we make a space in our society for everybody?
我们如何在社会中为每个人留出一席之地？

